WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 7.3: Checkpoints and PowerShell Direct. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 7.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.850
3, Checkpoints and PowerShell Direct.

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.500
In this topic you manage virtual machines
from the Hyper-V host.

4
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:14.750
You will see how a checkpoint works with
AVHDX files, and how to choose between

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.600
production and standard checkpoints.

6
00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:22.450
You will create, apply, rename and delete
checkpoints, and learn why a checkpoint is

7
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:23.900
not a backup.

8
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:29.350
Then you run commands inside a VM with
PowerShell Direct, copy files with

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.600
Copy-VMFile, turn on nested
virtualization, and compare two ways to

10
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.050
team network adapters.

11
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:42.500
A checkpoint saves the state of a VM at
one moment, so you can go back to it

12
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:43.950
later.

13
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.400
Here is how.

14
00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.050
VM1 already uses a differencing disk on
the read-only base disk.

15
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:55.900
When you take a checkpoint, Hyper-V
freezes VM1 dot vhdx and creates a new

16
00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.550
differencing file with the extension
avhdx.

17
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.800
Every new write goes there.

18
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.050
The AVHDX sits in the same folder as the
VHDX.

19
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:11.300
Each new checkpoint adds a link to the
chain, and every link needs its parent.

20
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.150
So never delete one of these files by
hand.

21
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.400
Hyper-V has two checkpoint types.

22
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.050
A production checkpoint is the default.

23
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.500
It uses backup technology inside the
guest, the Volume Shadow Copy Service in

24
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.950
Windows, so the data on disk is
consistent.

25
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.600
It does not save memory, so after you
apply it, the VM is off and boots

26
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.050
normally.

27
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.900
A standard checkpoint also saves the
memory and device state, so the VM comes

28
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.550
back running, open programs and all.

29
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.600
Microsoft warns that standard checkpoints
can cause data problems for servers that

30
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.450
copy data to each other, such as domain
controllers.

31
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.900
You set the checkpoint type for each VM.

32
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:02.550
In Hyper-V Manager, right-click the VM,
select Settings, and under Management

33
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.000
select Checkpoints.

34
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.650
Enable checkpoints is on by default.

35
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.500
Production checkpoints is selected, and
under it a check box lets Hyper-V make a

36
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.350
standard checkpoint when a production
checkpoint is not possible.

37
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.000
You can also choose standard checkpoints.

38
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.850
Checkpoint File Location says where the
checkpoint files go.

39
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.500
And Use automatic checkpoints makes one
each time the VM starts.

40
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.350
On Windows Server that option is off by
default.

41
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.000
The same settings live in PowerShell.

42
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.650
Get-VM, piped to Select-Object, shows the
checkpoint type and whether automatic

43
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.100
checkpoints are on.

44
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.150
For VM1 you see Production and False.

45
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.200
Set-VM with dash CheckpointType changes
the type.

46
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.050
Standard saves memory too.

47
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.900
Production falls back to standard when it
has to.

48
00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:56.150
ProductionOnly fails instead of falling
back, and Disabled blocks checkpoints

49
00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.600
completely.

50
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.250
Set-VM with dash
AutomaticCheckpointsEnabled dollar false

51
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.300
turns off automatic checkpoints for that
VM.

52
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:08.750
In the lab you only read these settings
and keep VM1 on Production.

53
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.600
In Hyper-V Manager, select a VM and the
Checkpoints pane shows its checkpoints as

54
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.050
a tree.

55
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.700
Each one sits under its parent.

56
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.950
The item called Now shows where the VM
runs today.

57
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.200
To take a checkpoint, select Checkpoint in
the Actions pane.

58
00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.250
When you select a checkpoint, the Actions
pane offers Apply, Rename, Delete

59
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.500
Checkpoint and Delete Checkpoint Subtree.

60
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:39.950
A new checkpoint is named after the VM,
with the date and time.

61
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.000
Rename it to something useful, like
Before-Change, so everyone knows why it

62
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:46.450
exists.

63
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:51.900
When you apply a checkpoint, the Apply
Checkpoint dialog asks what to do.

64
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:56.950
Create Checkpoint and Apply first saves
the current state, then goes back.

65
00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.200
That is the safe choice.

66
00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:05.050
Apply just goes back, and the changes
since then are lost with no undo.

67
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.300
Revert is a VM action that applies the
last checkpoint.

68
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:14.350
Delete Checkpoint does not lose your
current data: Hyper-V merges the AVHDX

69
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.800
into its parent and then removes the file,
while the VM keeps running.

70
00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:24.050
Delete Checkpoint Subtree removes a
checkpoint and everything after it.

71
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:28.700
In PowerShell, Checkpoint-VM takes a
checkpoint, and dash SnapshotName gives it

72
00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.150
a name.

73
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.000
Notice the old word.

74
00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:37.450
Checkpoints used to be called snapshots,
and most cmdlet names still say snapshot.

75
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.500
Get-VMSnapshot lists the checkpoints with
their parents.

76
00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.950
Restore-VMSnapshot applies one, like Apply
in Hyper-V Manager.

77
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.400
Rename-VMSnapshot renames one, and
Remove-VMSnapshot deletes one, which

78
00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:48.850
starts the merge.

79
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:54.700
Aliases with the newer word also work,
such as Get-VMCheckpoint, so you may see

80
00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.150
both in documentation.

81
00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.800
In the lab you use both Hyper-V Manager
and these cmdlets.

82
00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:05.850
Here is a rule worth remembering: a
checkpoint is not a backup.

83
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:13.300
The AVHDX files sit on the same host and
the same disk as the VM, so one failed

84
00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:14.750
disk takes everything.

85
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.000
A checkpoint holds only the changes since
its parent, so it is useless without the

86
00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:22.450
chain.

87
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:28.700
The files keep growing, long chains slow
the disk, and old checkpoints can fill a

88
00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.150
volume.

89
00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.400
And going back in time confuses servers
that replicate data.

90
00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:39.850
Use checkpoints for short changes, then
delete them, and protect servers with real

91
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.700
backups on other storage.

92
00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:47.150
PowerShell Direct lets you run PowerShell
inside a VM from its Hyper-V host.

93
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.800
It does not use the network.

94
00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.450
It talks to the guest through Hyper-V
itself, over the VMBus.

95
00:05:54.700 --> 00:06:01.900
So it works even if the VM has no network
adapter, a wrong IP address, or a firewall

96
00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.750
that blocks remote management.

97
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:09.200
The requirements: host and guest run
Windows 10, Windows Server 2016 or later.

98
00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:15.050
You run it on the host that runs the VM,
as a Hyper-V administrator.

99
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:21.300
The VM must be running, and you need
credentials for an account in the guest.

100
00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:23.550
First, store the guest's credentials.

101
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.800
Get-Credential with VM1 backslash
Administrator opens a dialog for the

102
00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.050
password of VM1's own Administrator
account, not your domain account.

103
00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:37.500
Invoke-Command with dash VMName runs one
command or script block inside VM1 and

104
00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:38.950
then closes.

105
00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.800
Here, hostname returns VM1.

106
00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:44.250
Enter-PSSession with dash VMName starts an
interactive session.

107
00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:50.100
The prompt changes to VM1 in square
brackets, so you always know where your

108
00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:51.550
commands run.

109
00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.600
Exit-PSSession takes you back to the host.

110
00:06:54.850 --> 00:07:00.850
In the lab you first rename the guest to
VM1, so this account name works.

111
00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:05.100
Copy-VMFile copies a file from the host
into a guest.

112
00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:10.150
It needs the Guest Service Interface
integration service, which is off by

113
00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.600
default, so turn it on first with
Enable-VMIntegrationService.

114
00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:19.850
Then Copy-VMFile takes the VM name, a
source path on the host, and a destination

115
00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.700
path in the guest.

116
00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:28.350
Dash FileSource Host is required, and it
is the only value, so the copy only goes

117
00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.200
from host to guest.

118
00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:35.250
Dash CreateFullPath creates the folder in
the guest if it is missing.

119
00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:39.100
Then PowerShell Direct can read the file
inside VM1.

120
00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:42.950
Nested virtualization means running
Hyper-V inside a virtual machine.

121
00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.200
Our pod is an example.

122
00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:50.650
HV01 is itself a VM in NetLab, and NetLab
hands the processor's virtualization

123
00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.500
features through to it.

124
00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.550
HV01 could do the same for VM1.

125
00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:03.000
You need an Intel processor with VT-x and
EPT, or a recent AMD EPYC or Ryzen with a

126
00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:04.450
newer host version.

127
00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.300
You turn it on while the VM is off.

128
00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:14.150
While Hyper-V runs inside a VM, that VM's
memory stays fixed, even with Dynamic

129
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:15.600
Memory.

130
00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:21.050
And nested VMs need MAC address spoofing
or NAT to reach the network.

131
00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:24.100
Here are the commands on the host.

132
00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:29.150
First, Stop-VM, because the processor
setting cannot change while the VM runs.

133
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.200
Then Set-VMProcessor with dash
ExposeVirtualizationExtensions dollar true

134
00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:35.250
passes the virtualization features to the
guest.

135
00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:38.300
Next, Get-VMNetworkAdapter piped to
Set-VMNetworkAdapter with dash

136
00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:42.950
MacAddressSpoofing On lets frames from
nested VMs pass through VM1's adapter.

137
00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.600
Get-VMProcessor confirms the setting is
True.

138
00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.450
Then start the VM.

139
00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:53.300
The next step would be installing Hyper-V
inside VM1, but that needs more memory

140
00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:55.550
than our host can spare.

141
00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:00.600
A host with one network adapter is a
single point of failure.

142
00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:04.850
Teaming joins several adapters, so traffic
continues if one fails.

143
00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:10.300
For Hyper-V hosts, Microsoft uses Switch
Embedded Teaming, or SET, which is built

144
00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:12.150
into the Hyper-V switch.

145
00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:17.600
It takes up to eight identical adapters
and works in switch independent mode.

146
00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.650
The older LBFO NIC Teaming from Server
Manager is deprecated for Hyper-V.

147
00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:28.900
From Windows Server 2022 on, binding a
Hyper-V switch to an LBFO team is blocked

148
00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:30.350
by default.

149
00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:35.400
You create a SET switch with New-VMSwitch,
two adapter names, and dash

150
00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:36.850
EnableEmbeddedTeaming dollar true.

151
00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.700
HV01 has one adapter, so this stays a
concept.

152
00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:42.550
Four mistakes to avoid.

153
00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.600
One: keeping checkpoints as the only
backup.

154
00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:51.450
Back up to other storage, and delete each
checkpoint once the change is tested.

155
00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.500
Two: deleting AVHDX files in File
Explorer.

156
00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:59.150
Delete checkpoints in Hyper-V Manager or
with Remove-VMSnapshot, so Hyper-V merges

157
00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:00.600
them.

158
00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:05.250
Three: PowerShell Direct with the wrong
credentials or a stopped VM.

159
00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:10.700
Run it on the host, start the VM, and use
the guest's account.

160
00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:16.150
Four: exposing virtualization on a running
VM, or forgetting the network for nested

161
00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:17.600
VMs.

162
00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:21.850
Stop the VM first, then turn on MAC
address spoofing.

163
00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.700
Time to check yourself.

164
00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:25.550
VM1 uses production checkpoints.

165
00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:31.000
You took Before-Change, created C colon
Lab proof dot txt, and then took

166
00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:32.450
After-Change.

167
00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.300
First, you apply Before-Change.

168
00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:38.950
What state is VM1 in, and is proof dot txt
there?

169
00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.200
Second, you delete both checkpoints.

170
00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:43.850
What happens to the AVHDX files?

171
00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.500
Third, VM1's network adapter is
disconnected.

172
00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:51.550
Can Invoke-Command with dash VMName still
work, and what does it need?

173
00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:55.800
Fourth, a new host has two identical 25
gigabit adapters.

174
00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:59.650
How do you team them for the Hyper-V
switch?

175
00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:01.900
Pause the video and answer.

176
00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:06.150
One: VM1 is off, and proof dot txt is
gone.

177
00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:12.800
A production checkpoint has no memory
state, so you start VM1 and it is back to

178
00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:14.650
before the file existed.

179
00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:20.100
Two: Hyper-V merges the AVHDX files into
their parents and then removes them.

180
00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:23.150
VM1 runs on VM1 dot vhdx again.

181
00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.400
Three: yes, because PowerShell Direct does
not use the network.

182
00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:33.250
Run it on HV01 as an administrator, with
VM1 running and VM1's own Administrator

183
00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:34.700
credentials.

184
00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:39.750
Four: use SET, with New-VMSwitch and dash
EnableEmbeddedTeaming dollar true, not an

185
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.200
LBFO team.

186
00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:43.850
Let me show you on HV01.

187
00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:47.300
VM1's settings show production checkpoints
with the fallback.

188
00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.750
I take a checkpoint and rename it
Before-Change.

189
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.200
Inside VM1 I create proof dot txt, then
take After-Change with Checkpoint-VM, and

190
00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:58.050
Get-VMSnapshot shows the tree.

191
00:11:58.300 --> 00:11:59.900
Now I apply Before-Change.

192
00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:01.350
VM1 is off.

193
00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.800
I start it, and the file is gone.

194
00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:09.450
I delete both checkpoints, and VM1 is back
on its VHDX.

195
00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.100
Last, I disconnect VM1's network adapter.

196
00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:15.550
Invoke-Command still returns VM1, and
Enter-PSSession still opens.

197
00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:17.000
No network needed.

198
00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:18.450
Let's sum up.

199
00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:23.900
A checkpoint freezes the disk, and new
writes go to an AVHDX file.

200
00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:28.950
Production checkpoints are the default and
use VSS; standard checkpoints save memory

201
00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:30.400
too.

202
00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:35.850
Deleting a checkpoint merges its AVHDX,
and a checkpoint is never a backup.

203
00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:40.900
PowerShell Direct uses dash VMName and the
guest's credentials, with no network

204
00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.350
needed.

205
00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.000
Copy-VMFile needs the Guest Service
Interface.

206
00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:50.050
For nested virtualization, expose the
extensions with the VM off, and team

207
00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:51.900
Hyper-V adapters with SET.

208
00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:53.350
In Lab 7.

209
00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:59.200
3, about 75 minutes and practice only, you
do all of this on HV01.
